<% @title = "Privacy" %>

<%# The ids email_address, full_address, postal_answer, public_request and
    real_name are linked to from core's request forms and must stay
    (bundle exec rake themes:check_help_sections). %>
<%= page_band %>
<%= render :partial => 'sidebar' %>

<div id="left_column_flip" class="left_column_flip">

  <p>This is a demonstration site with invented data. A real site replaces
    this page with its own privacy notice, naming who runs it and the
    services it uses.</p>

  <h2 id="readers">If you are reading this site</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt id="tracking">Does this site track me? <a href="#tracking" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>No. There's no advertising or analytics tracking. The server keeps
        ordinary logs, such as the address a page was requested from, for a
        short time to keep the site working and secure.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="cookies">Does it use cookies? <a href="#cookies" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Only the ones the site needs: one to keep you signed in, and one that
        protects forms from being sent by other sites.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="contact_messages">What happens to a message I send through the contact page? <a href="#contact_messages" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>It's emailed to the people who run the site, and kept only as long as
        it takes to deal with it.</p>
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="named">If you are named in a request</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt id="names">Why does my name appear? <a href="#names" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Requests and their correspondence are private, so your name appears
        publicly only if it's in a document that an administrator checked and
        published.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="takedown">Can something about me be removed? <a href="#takedown" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Yes. Use the <a href="<%= help_contact_path %>">contact page</a> and
        say which document. A published document can be withdrawn, and its
        files erased.</p>
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="documents_section">Documents</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt id="documents">What is published as a document? <a href="#documents" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>A file from an authority's reply, with a title, a date and a short
        description. Before publishing, an administrator checks every page,
        and anything that shouldn't be public is redacted.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="document_files">How long are document files kept? <a href="#document_files" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>The published file is kept while the document is published. The
        original as received, and versions replaced by a redacted one, are
        erased after a set period.</p>
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="services">Who handles information for this site</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt id="hosting">Where is the site hosted? <a href="#hosting" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>A real site names its hosting provider here.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="email">Who handles email? <a href="#email" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Requests and replies pass through the site's own mail server. A real
        site names any email provider it uses here.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="ai">Is anything read by AI? <a href="#ai" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>If the site turns it on, replies to requests can be read by an AI
        service, which says whether a reply needs the requester or is
        routine, and what it decided. The reading only helps sort replies:
        it never sends anything to an authority, and the requester can
        change what it says. In this demonstration the readings are stored
        with the sample data and no service is used.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="transfers">Does information leave the UK? <a href="#transfers" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>A real site says here whether any service it uses is outside the
        UK, and what protects the information.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="basis">Why is this allowed? <a href="#basis" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>A real site gives its lawful basis for using personal information
        here.</p>
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <% if @user %>
  <h2 id="requester">If you are the requester</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt id="public_request">Is my request public? <a href="#public_request" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>No. Your account, requests and correspondence stay private. Only
        documents an administrator has checked and published are public, and
        publishing one doesn't publish your request.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="email_address">Who sees my email address? <a href="#email_address" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Nobody. Each request is sent from its own address at this site, and
        the authority's replies come back to that address. Your own email
        address is used only to tell you about your requests.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="real_name">Do I have to use my real name? <a href="#real_name" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Yes, for a request under the Freedom of Information Act. A request
        has to give the name of the person making it, and an authority can
        decline one made under a pseudonym.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="full_address">The authority has asked for my postal address <a href="#full_address" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>A request needs "an address for correspondence", and an email
        address is one. You can reply to the authority to say so.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="postal_answer">They can only reply on paper <a href="#postal_answer" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Give the authority a postal address privately, not in a message on
        this site. When the reply arrives you can scan it and add it to the
        request.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="requester_documents">Can documents from my request be published? <a href="#requester_documents" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Yes, once a document has been made from a reply and an administrator
        has checked it. If it names you, it's published only if you agree or
        redact your name. You can withdraw a published document at any time.</p>
    </dd>

    <dt id="ai_reading">Can I stop replies being read by AI? <a href="#ai_reading" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>Yes. Turn off <a href="<%= profile_ai_reading_path %>">reading replies
        with AI</a>, which is linked from your profile page. New replies are
        then never sent to the AI service. Each one is shown as needing your
        attention, and you say what it did with your request. Replies read
        before you turn it off keep their status.</p>
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <% end %>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt id="your_rights">What can I ask for? <a href="#your_rights" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></dt>
    <dd>
      <p>You can ask for a copy of the information the site holds about you,
        and ask for it to be corrected or deleted, for its use to be limited,
        or object to its use. Ask through the
        <a href="<%= help_contact_path %>">contact page</a>.</p>
      <p>If you're unhappy with how information about you is handled, tell
        the people who run the site first. You can also complain to the data
        protection regulator.</p>
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <div id="hash_link_padding"></div>
</div>
